WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.395
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:11.660 --> 00:00:14.200
I guess how to get a data-driven
learning-based approach

00:00:14.200 --> 00:00:16.688
to work on real robots.

00:00:16.688 --> 00:00:17.230
I don't know.

00:00:17.230 --> 00:00:22.090
I mean, I guess AGI is one
of these things that's like,

00:00:22.090 --> 00:00:25.030
if you did solve it, it
would be truly a landmark.

00:00:25.030 --> 00:00:29.440
I guess in my field, that
is the absolute holy grail.

00:00:29.440 --> 00:00:31.270
And people have
theorems that show

00:00:31.270 --> 00:00:34.720
that these kinds of
AGI systems might even

00:00:34.720 --> 00:00:37.510
exist in certain ways.

00:00:37.510 --> 00:00:38.620
But others debate it.

00:00:38.620 --> 00:00:42.145
Others say that you might
never get an AGI system.

00:00:42.145 --> 00:00:44.020
But I think that something
along those lines,

00:00:44.020 --> 00:00:46.090
at least in the field
of machine learning,

00:00:46.090 --> 00:00:48.940
would be one of the
greatest unsolved problems

00:00:48.940 --> 00:00:50.350
that you could tackle.

00:00:50.350 --> 00:00:53.980
My interests often
fall into places

00:00:53.980 --> 00:00:58.750
where technology and humans
interact and have overlap.

00:00:58.750 --> 00:01:03.070
And I think that this is going
to be true regardless of where

00:01:03.070 --> 00:01:10.390
and when we are, but creating
equitable spaces in technology

00:01:10.390 --> 00:01:14.620
that do minimal harm
and give people voices.

00:01:14.620 --> 00:01:18.310
I think that across the
board, that's very difficult.

00:01:18.310 --> 00:01:20.800
The current place that we see
that the most right now, I

00:01:20.800 --> 00:01:22.220
think, is the machine learning.

00:01:22.220 --> 00:01:23.650
So machine learning,
in fairness,

00:01:23.650 --> 00:01:26.470
is really, really hot.

00:01:26.470 --> 00:01:28.540
But there are other
places where that's true.

00:01:28.540 --> 00:01:30.820
If you think about
just user experiences,

00:01:30.820 --> 00:01:33.760
if you think about
your iPhone, the needs

00:01:33.760 --> 00:01:38.020
of someone who is much older,
who's in a geriatric facility,

00:01:38.020 --> 00:01:42.550
for example, are significantly
different than what someone

00:01:42.550 --> 00:01:44.800
in their 20s or 40s or 50s.

00:01:44.800 --> 00:01:47.320
I think one of the most
important unsolved problems,

00:01:47.320 --> 00:01:50.140
but also possibly
unsolvable problems,

00:01:50.140 --> 00:01:53.140
is figuring out
how to get machines

00:01:53.140 --> 00:01:57.730
to better interact with humans
or how to develop the machinery

00:01:57.730 --> 00:01:59.560
to understand how
humans will interact

00:01:59.560 --> 00:02:02.650
with machines or algorithms.

00:02:02.650 --> 00:02:06.220
I think that we're starting
to use incredibly complicated

00:02:06.220 --> 00:02:08.300
systems all over the place.

00:02:08.300 --> 00:02:10.720
And to me, the
complicated problem

00:02:10.720 --> 00:02:13.960
is the integration of
these complex systems that

00:02:13.960 --> 00:02:17.050
have bugs and problems
with human beings

00:02:17.050 --> 00:02:20.170
because humans, ultimately, are
the ones who are using the code

00:02:20.170 --> 00:02:23.890
and making decisions based
off of the recommendations

00:02:23.890 --> 00:02:25.120
that the code gives them.

00:02:25.120 --> 00:02:29.050
And I think thinking about
how code interacts with people

00:02:29.050 --> 00:02:32.750
is just such an open question.

00:02:32.750 --> 00:02:37.390
So when I think of this question
in terms of the hot topics

00:02:37.390 --> 00:02:41.410
these days, machine learning
and artificial intelligence,

00:02:41.410 --> 00:02:45.490
most of the stuff is
limited by our capabilities

00:02:45.490 --> 00:02:49.510
as humans because, especially
for supervised learning

00:02:49.510 --> 00:02:52.420
methods, we need to provide
some sort of data set.

00:02:52.420 --> 00:02:55.610
And that data set is
usually labeled by humans.

00:02:55.610 --> 00:02:59.740
So if you want to go beyond
that and explore intelligence

00:02:59.740 --> 00:03:01.780
beyond human
intelligence, then we

00:03:01.780 --> 00:03:04.690
need to think about new
ways, different ways,

00:03:04.690 --> 00:03:07.480
that we might not even
be able to perceive.

00:03:07.480 --> 00:03:10.660
So I think that's going
to be the next challenge

00:03:10.660 --> 00:03:12.610
in the next decades.

00:03:12.610 --> 00:03:15.580
Understanding when your
model is no longer going

00:03:15.580 --> 00:03:18.850
to work before seeing
labels for that is,

00:03:18.850 --> 00:03:20.100
I think, incredibly important.

00:03:20.100 --> 00:03:22.017
We have lots of tools
to say, if I know this--

00:03:22.017 --> 00:03:24.660
if I see the data comes out
of this kind of machine,

00:03:24.660 --> 00:03:26.410
make the model work
while given that data.

00:03:26.410 --> 00:03:28.868
But what happens in the wild
when things arbitrarily change

00:03:28.868 --> 00:03:30.470
is still very hard
to understand.

00:03:30.470 --> 00:03:32.470
And I think is a key
bottleneck to really making

00:03:32.470 --> 00:03:34.480
intelligent systems
society scale.

00:03:34.480 --> 00:03:38.440
So SafeML, I think, is
still open, fun challenge.

00:03:38.440 --> 00:03:38.940
Yeah.

00:03:38.940 --> 00:03:41.273
I think what's interesting
in my particular field, which

00:03:41.273 --> 00:03:45.670
is about how to make robots,
is that there isn't really

00:03:45.670 --> 00:03:48.940
one existing problem that
everyone is trying to solve.

00:03:48.940 --> 00:03:53.200
It's more that people
are trying to figure out

00:03:53.200 --> 00:03:57.490
new problems to define and then
proceed to solve them that way.

00:03:57.490 --> 00:04:00.840
[MUSIC PLAYING]

